<DocsLayout title="Evidence & gates" description="Turn data, model, reliability, and release claims into reviewable evidence." current="evidence">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Proof before promotion</p>
  <h1>Make the claim.<br /><em>Show the evidence.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">A green training run is not a release decision. super-charge-ml records the raw inputs, reproducible command, artifact, reviewer, limitations, and disposition behind each claim.</p>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>The rule:</strong> implemented means code exists; supported means the declared lifecycle passes; production-proven means an independent reviewer reproduced the evidence from a clean checkout.</p></div>

  <h2>One claim, one evidence record</h2>
  <p>Every release criterion is a named record. Do not write “quality checked” when the useful claim is “the curation report contains a structured finding for every detected issue.” The record should make the claim falsifiable.</p>
  <pre><code>{`criterion_id:
  claim: structured curation findings are persisted
  scope: {release: beta, area: data}
  raw_inputs: {dataset: sha256:...}
  command_or_test: uv run pytest tests/integration/test_quality_checks_in_run.py
  artifact: {path: curation_report.json, digest: sha256:...}
  result: pass
  reviewer: independent-reviewer
  observed_at: 2026-10-20T12:00:00+00:00
  limitations: [connector object hashes are not yet raw-byte hashes]
  decision: accepted`}</code></pre>

  <h2>How the gates work</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Declare the boundary:</strong> a capability names its modality, task, model family, evaluation, uncertainty, fairness, explainability, and inference surface.</li>
    <li><strong>Collect raw evidence:</strong> keep dataset snapshots, test output, artifact digests, resource measurements, and failure traces.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluate the claim:</strong> compare results with explicit thresholds, uncertainty, subgroup warnings, and known limitations.</li>
    <li><strong>Review independently:</strong> reproduce from a clean checkout and recompute at least one critical result.</li>
    <li><strong>Promote or demote:</strong> accept the capability, block the release, or mark it extension-only. Never hide an incomplete stage.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What blocks a release</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A supported modality is missing evidence for any of its 15 lifecycle stages.</li>
    <li>A required metric, diagnostic, fairness result, uncertainty result, or explanation is absent.</li>
    <li>A fault scenario has no verified safe outcome or recovery evidence.</li>
    <li>A dataset snapshot, model bundle, or dependency cannot be identified and reproduced.</li>
    <li>A reviewer cannot distinguish a warning from a release-blocking finding.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Generated controls are not approvals</h2>
  <p>Run artifacts make review possible, but they do not replace review. A dataset card starts as <code>machine_generated_unreviewed</code>; the fault-injection plan starts as <code>planned</code> and release-blocking; and a missing evaluation policy produces <code>policy_not_declared</code>. Those states are safer than inventing a threshold or implying that a checklist was executed.</p>

  <h2>Independent review is a separate proof</h2>
  <p><code>release_evidence.json</code> is itself versioned and HMAC-signed when <code>SCML_SIGNING_KEY</code> is configured. The release evaluator verifies that signature before accepting the evidence index, and the run lineage records its digest. This protects the evidence collection from being edited after the run; it does not replace signer custody, key rotation, or independent review.</p>
  <p><code>IndependentReleaseReview</code> is the signed record for the final human or external review. It binds the reviewer to the exact source commit, clean-clone and environment digests, reproducible commands, artifact digests, and independently recomputed metric, fairness, and explanation claims. The reviewer must not appear in the implementer set, every claim must pass, and the decision must be approved. A valid record proves the review contract; it does not manufacture an external reviewer or turn a local rehearsal into production evidence.</p>
  <pre><code>{`review.accepted(
    source_commit="&lt;exact-release-sha&gt;",
    required_kinds=("metric", "fairness", "explanation"),
    signing_key="&lt;review-key&gt;",
)`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Read the evidence in order</h2>
  <div class="diagram">
    <div class="diagram-caption"><span>Evidence path</span><strong>raw source to release decision</strong></div>
    <div class="data-path">
      <div class="path-step highlighted"><span>01</span><b>Raw source</b></div><i class="path-line"></i>
      <div class="path-step"><span>02</span><b>Snapshot</b></div><i class="path-line"></i>
      <div class="path-step"><span>03</span><b>Run & bundle</b></div><i class="path-line"></i>
      <div class="path-step"><span>04</span><b>Evaluation</b></div><i class="path-line"></i>
      <div class="path-step highlighted"><span>05</span><b>Review</b></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The run also carries a lineage graph</h2>
  <p>Completed packages write <code>evidence_graph.json</code>. It is a deterministic, content-linked DAG rather than a second copy of the event log: each node names an identity and each edge says how one artifact produced or constrained the next. Serving can append an inference node only when the event names the exact bundle digest recorded by the graph.</p>
  <pre><code>{`raw-source → snapshot → curation → split → model
                                      ├→ evaluation → explanations
                                      └→ bundle → inference event

The graph digest changes when an identity or relationship changes.`}</code></pre>
  <p><code>InferenceEvidenceLinker</code> checks the graph digest, checks the bundle digest, and writes the updated graph atomically. Replaying the same event is safe; reusing an event ID for different input or output evidence fails closed. Use the graph to answer “which snapshot and curation decision produced this bundle and served this event?” Use the event log to answer “what happened, and in what order?” Both are necessary; neither is a release approval on its own.</p>

  <p class="meta">Evidence is a product surface, not an appendix · 0.1 beta</p>
